WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.214
Can a loop recover one value?

00:00:02.214 --> 00:00:04.134
Take the function omega squared.

00:00:04.134 --> 00:00:08.464
Our target is its value at x equal to one half, namely one quarter.

00:00:08.464 --> 00:00:12.496
Cauchy's formula recovers this number from a counterclockwise circuit.

00:00:12.796 --> 00:00:14.451
Place a pole at the target.

00:00:14.451 --> 00:00:17.160
Divide the function by omega minus x.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:21.810
The denominator vanishes at one half, while the numerator there is one quarter.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.602
This gives a genuine simple pole.

00:00:23.902 --> 00:00:25.494
Start with a small circle.

00:00:25.494 --> 00:00:26.859
Its centre is x.

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:30.527
Take its radius epsilon to be one quarter for the drawing.

00:00:30.527 --> 00:00:36.778
Any sufficiently small positive radius works if the circle and its interior lie inside the ellipse.

00:00:37.078 --> 00:00:39.566
The tangent cancels the denominator.

00:00:39.566 --> 00:00:44.194
On the circle, omega minus x is epsilon times e to the i t.

00:00:44.194 --> 00:00:48.695
The differential contributes i times that same factor times d t.

00:00:48.695 --> 00:00:54.903
After dividing by two pi i, only the function value times d t over two pi remains.

00:00:55.203 --> 00:00:56.752
Keep the constant part.

00:00:56.752 --> 00:00:58.757
Expand the square along the circle.

00:00:58.757 --> 00:01:02.810
One term is constant, one turns once, and another turns twice.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.500
Both rotating terms average to zero over a full turn.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.081
What survives is x squared, or one quarter.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.022
What does the residue measure?

00:01:12.022 --> 00:01:15.883
Look at omega squared divided by omega minus x.

00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:18.293
Only the denominator vanishes at x.

00:01:18.293 --> 00:01:23.946
For this simple pole, multiply by omega minus x and let omega approach x.

00:01:23.946 --> 00:01:26.313
The remaining numerator tends to x squared.

00:01:26.313 --> 00:01:29.363
At x equal to one half, that gives one quarter.

00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:32.947
This is the coefficient of one over omega minus x.

00:01:33.247 --> 00:01:35.244
Blend two points at the same angle.

00:01:35.244 --> 00:01:36.630
Fix the angle t.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:40.768
Mark the point on the small circle and the matching point on the ellipse.

00:01:40.768 --> 00:01:44.544
The first term takes one minus lambda times the circle point.

00:01:44.544 --> 00:01:47.295
The second takes lambda times the ellipse point.

00:01:47.295 --> 00:01:50.388
At lambda zero, the sum gives the circle point.

00:01:50.388 --> 00:01:52.499
At one half, it is their midpoint.

00:01:52.499 --> 00:01:54.803
At one, it reaches the ellipse point.

00:01:54.803 --> 00:01:56.210
Now let t turn.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:59.090
The blended point traces the loop called C lambda.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.472
Keep the pole inside every loop.

00:02:01.472 --> 00:02:03.712
Each intermediate loop is an ellipse.

00:02:03.712 --> 00:02:05.524
Look at its two real tips.

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:09.556
The left one stays to the left of x, and the right one stays to the right.

00:02:09.556 --> 00:02:14.974
As t increases through one turn, every loop goes counterclockwise once around the pole.

00:02:14.974 --> 00:02:22.142
Cauchy's formula therefore gives the same normalized integral, one quarter, for every lambda from zero to one.

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:24.673
The tangent supplies the differential.

00:02:24.673 --> 00:02:27.574
Follow the point upward at the right tip of the ellipse.

00:02:27.574 --> 00:02:29.728
At the top the tangent points left.

00:02:29.728 --> 00:02:31.882
Advance the angle by a small amount.

00:02:31.882 --> 00:02:35.978
The short chord follows the tangent more closely as the step shrinks.

00:02:35.978 --> 00:02:41.097
The differential is the tangent, C prime of t, times the angle step, d t.

00:02:41.097 --> 00:02:44.062
Both its direction and its speed enter the integral.

00:02:44.362 --> 00:02:46.551
Multiply the two complex arrows.

00:02:46.551 --> 00:02:51.692
The first arrow represents the quotient, omega squared over omega minus x.

00:02:51.692 --> 00:02:54.529
The second is the tangent at the moving point.

00:02:54.529 --> 00:02:59.200
Complex multiplication adds their angles and multiplies their lengths.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:06.261
Dividing by two pi i turns the product clockwise by a quarter turn and scales it by one over two pi.

00:03:06.261 --> 00:03:09.140
Multiply this rate by the small angle step.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.722
Put the resulting arrow at the tip of the running sum.

00:03:12.022 --> 00:03:14.595
On the small circle, the factors cancel.

00:03:14.595 --> 00:03:18.541
The radius arrow from x is epsilon times e to the i t.

00:03:18.541 --> 00:03:21.164
The tangent is i times that same arrow.

00:03:21.164 --> 00:03:23.873
Divide the tangent by the radius arrow.

00:03:23.873 --> 00:03:26.965
Their lengths cancel, and the quarter turn leaves i.

00:03:26.965 --> 00:03:29.760
That i cancels the i in the normalization.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.133
What remains is the circle value squared, times d t over two pi.

00:03:34.433 --> 00:03:37.219
Keep the constant and turn the other two waves.

00:03:37.219 --> 00:03:38.563
Expand the square.

00:03:38.563 --> 00:03:42.722
The constant arrow has length x squared, one quarter, and points right.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:47.628
The next term has length two x epsilon, also one quarter, and turns once.

00:03:47.628 --> 00:03:52.065
The last has length epsilon squared, one sixteenth, and turns twice.

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:56.608
Each rotating arrow meets an equal opposite arrow during its own cycle.

00:03:56.608 --> 00:03:58.591
Both full-turn averages vanish.

00:03:58.591 --> 00:04:00.297
Only the constant survives.

00:04:00.297 --> 00:04:03.049
Its average over the full circle is one quarter.

00:04:03.349 --> 00:04:05.453
Build the partial integral from zero.

00:04:05.453 --> 00:04:10.167
A sub C of T adds the little arrows from angle zero to angle T.

00:04:10.167 --> 00:04:14.135
Before the first step, the interval is empty, so the sum is zero.

00:04:14.135 --> 00:04:17.803
Halfway around the small circle the real part is one eighth.

00:04:17.803 --> 00:04:20.747
The imaginary part is one over four pi.

00:04:20.747 --> 00:04:22.410
The integral is still moving.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.497
Finish the turn.

00:04:23.497 --> 00:04:28.063
The imaginary contributions cancel and the real part reaches one quarter.

00:04:28.363 --> 00:04:31.405
The two signed heights give one complex integral.

00:04:31.405 --> 00:04:32.791
Return to the ellipse.

00:04:32.791 --> 00:04:38.764
Read the real part of the rate as height on the first boundary and the imaginary part on the second.

00:04:38.764 --> 00:04:43.840
Each small angle interval contributes its signed height times its angular width.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.747
Watch those two contributions move the running sum horizontally and vertically.

00:04:48.747 --> 00:04:52.757
The ellipse and the small circle take different routes in the value plane.

00:04:52.757 --> 00:04:55.402
Both finish at one quarter on the real axis.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:58.172
Now recover a coefficient.

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:02.630
A Legendre coefficient combines values from the whole real interval.

00:05:02.630 --> 00:05:08.518
For degree two, multiply x squared by P two of x and integrate from minus one to one.

00:05:08.818 --> 00:05:10.495
Include the normalization.

00:05:10.495 --> 00:05:14.910
The classical Legendre polynomial equals one at the right endpoint.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:18.899
Its coefficient formula carries a factor of n plus one half.

00:05:18.899 --> 00:05:21.459
For degree two, that factor is five halves.

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:23.350
Keep the sign of the weight.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:27.126
P two is one half of the quantity three x squared minus one.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:30.901
Its zeros are plus and minus one over the square root of three.

00:05:30.901 --> 00:05:35.979
The product is negative between them except at zero, and positive toward both ends.

00:05:36.279 --> 00:05:37.764
Add the signed areas.

00:05:37.764 --> 00:05:41.518
Integrating x squared times P two gives four fifteenths.

00:05:41.518 --> 00:05:45.421
Multiply by five halves and the coefficient is two thirds.

00:05:45.421 --> 00:05:48.493
This is the number we will now recover from the ellipse.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.705
Give every interval point its own kernel.

00:05:51.705 --> 00:05:56.462
Replace each f of x in the real integral by its Cauchy formula.

00:05:56.462 --> 00:06:01.454
As x moves along the interval, the factor one over omega minus x follows it.

00:06:01.754 --> 00:06:03.772
Exchange the two integrations.

00:06:03.772 --> 00:06:08.230
The ellipse boundary stays a positive distance from the entire interval.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:15.867
The parametrised integrand is continuous on a compact rectangle, so Fubini's theorem lets us integrate over x first.

00:06:16.167 --> 00:06:18.399
The kernels combine into one function.

00:06:18.399 --> 00:06:23.497
The bracket now integrates P two of x divided by omega minus x.

00:06:23.497 --> 00:06:29.492
By definition, half of that bracket is Q two of omega, so the whole bracket is twice Q two.

00:06:29.792 --> 00:06:32.109
Follow the coefficient around the ellipse.

00:06:32.109 --> 00:06:35.948
The factor of two from the bracket turns five halves into five.

00:06:35.948 --> 00:06:42.732
Multiply omega squared by Q two, by the oriented differential, and by five over two pi i.

00:06:42.732 --> 00:06:46.080
The accumulated value can be complex partway round.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.958
At the end it reaches two thirds.

00:06:48.258 --> 00:06:50.020
Keep the kernel off the slit.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:54.521
Q two is holomorphic off the real segment from minus one to one.

00:06:54.521 --> 00:06:57.955
The coefficient contour surrounds that entire segment.

00:06:57.955 --> 00:07:02.861
Deforming it across the slit would leave the region where this kernel is holomorphic.

